{
  "dom_id": "step-q27994",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(2-x)(1-x)^{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(2-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1,$ and\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(2-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/cc6ad46b678ec1fa600fb62c81114c6bf8695ac48dea83fad1380b566943b3e1.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
